= Symplectic geometry
{wiki}

Symplectic geometry studies smooth manifolds equipped with a closed nondegenerate differential two-form.

= Presymplectic form
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{title2=$d\omega=0,\quad\operatorname{rank}\omega=\mathrm{constant}$}

A <presymplectic form> is a closed <two-form>, commonly assumed to have constant rank, that need not be nondegenerate. A <symplectic form> is its nondegenerate special case. Its kernel distribution is integrable: for kernel fields $X,Y$, the identity $d\omega=0$ gives $\iota_{[X,Y]}\omega=0$. If the characteristic leaf quotient is smooth and the form descends, that quotient has a <symplectic form>. A regular <Hamiltonian> energy hypersurface carries such a form with kernel spanned by its <Hamiltonian vector field>.

= Symplectic capacity
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{title2=$c(X,\omega)$}

A symplectic capacity is monotone under <symplectic embeddings>, scales by $c(X,a\omega)=a\,c(X,\omega)$ for $a>0$, and has positive unit-ball capacity and finite unit-cylinder capacity. It is invariant under <symplectomorphisms>. A <normalized symplectic capacity> additionally assigns $\pi$ to both unit domains.

= Normalized symplectic capacity
{parent=Symplectic capacity}
{title2=$c(B^{2n}(1))=c(Z^{2n}(1))=\pi$}

A normalized <symplectic capacity> has the displayed unit-ball and unit-cylinder values. Dilation gives $\pi R^2$ for both radius-$R$ domains. Monotonicity then implies the sharp <Gromov non-squeezing theorem>.

= Eliashberg–Gromov rigidity theorem
{parent=Symplectic capacity}
{c}
{title2=$\operatorname{Symp}(M,\omega)\text{ is }C^0\text{-closed in }\operatorname{Diff}(M)$}

A locally uniform limit of <symplectomorphisms> that is a smooth diffeomorphism is symplectic. By <capacity preservation under uniform limits> and <linear capacity rigidity>, its derivative is symplectic or <anti-symplectic>. Repeat this argument after taking the product of every map with the identity on a symplectic plane. An anti-symplectic derivative on the original factor and a symplectic derivative on the added factor would give neither sign on the product, a contradiction. The argument uses <symplectic capacities> in the original and stabilized dimensions.

= Linear capacity rigidity
{parent=Symplectic capacity}
{title2=$c(TE)=c(E)\text{ for all ellipsoids}\implies T^*\omega=\pm\omega$}

An invertible real linear map preserving <symplectic capacities> of all <solid ellipsoids> is symplectic or <anti-symplectic>. For a pair with $\omega(u,v)=1$, if $0<|\omega(T^Tu,T^Tv)|<1$, complete this pair and its normalized transformed pair to <symplectic bases>. The resulting transpose matrix sends a unit ball into a <symplectic cylinder> of radius less than one, contradicting the capacity axioms after iterating the resulting linear map: its first coordinate pair contracts geometrically, while the image ellipsoid retains positive capacity. Perturbation handles a zero pairing; using $T^{-1}$ handles absolute value greater than one. Thus the absolute bilinear pairings agree. Their squares agree as polynomials, and the polynomial ring being an <integral domain> forces one global sign.

= Capacity preservation under uniform limits
{parent=Symplectic capacity}

Suppose diffeomorphisms preserving capacities of <solid ellipsoids> converge uniformly on compact sets to a diffeomorphism $\phi$. For $0<a<1$, uniform closeness and <degree of a continuous mapping> give $\phi_j(aE)\subseteq\phi(E)\subseteq\phi_j(a^{-1}E)$. The capacity axioms squeeze $c(\phi(E))$ between $a^2c(E)$ and $a^{-2}c(E)$. Letting $a$ tend to one proves equality. Rescaling a smooth $\phi$ near a point and repeating the argument also proves capacity preservation by its derivative.

= Geometric quantization
{parent=Symplectic geometry}

Geometric quantization associates quantum states and observables with a <symplectic manifold>. A first step uses a complex <line bundle> with a <connection on a vector bundle> whose curvature is proportional to the <symplectic form> divided by $\hbar$; its existence imposes an integrality condition. A real polarization is an integrable half-dimensional distribution with <Lagrangian submanifolds> as leaves. Requiring sections to be covariantly constant along the leaves reduces the variables to a configuration-space description. Restricting the curvature to a Lagrangian leaf gives zero, permitting local flat sections; global sections can require trivial holonomy. Exact Lagrangian graphs also describe local semiclassical phases through their generating functions.

= Polarization in geometric quantization
{parent=Geometric quantization}
{title2=$\mathcal F\subset T_{\mathbb C}P$}

= Geometric quantization polarization
{synonym}

A polarization is an involutive complex rank-half distribution on which the <symplectic form> vanishes. Polarized prequantum sections satisfy $\nabla_Xs=0$ for $X\in\mathcal F$. A real polarization is tangent to a <Lagrangian foliation>; a complex <Kähler> polarization uses the antiholomorphic tangent spaces. Only observables whose <Hamiltonian flows> preserve the chosen distribution act directly on the polarized space. Singular leaves, <holonomy> and changes of polarization are genuine quantization difficulties.

= Blattner-Kostant-Sternberg pairing
{c}
{parent=Polarization in geometric quantization}

= BKS pairing
{c}
{synonym}

The pairing compares state spaces belonging to two polarizations using their prequantum sections and half-forms. When it defines a suitable identification or projection, a classical <Hamiltonian flow> can transport a state to its transported polarization and the pairing can return it to the original state space. Differentiating this construction supplies operators for some observables that do not directly preserve the original polarization. Existence, domains and unitarity require additional hypotheses; it is not a universal canonical equivalence of all polarizations.

= Metaplectic correction
{parent=Polarization in geometric quantization}

= Half-form correction
{synonym}

Tensor the prequantum bundle with a square root of the canonical <determinant> bundle of a polarization, when such a square root exists. The half-form contributes to the state pairing and to the observable action. It improves coordinate invariance and accounts for familiar Maslov and oscillator zero-point shifts. Its existence is additional global data, not automatic from prequantum integrality.

= Prequantization
{parent=Geometric quantization}
{title2=$[\omega/(2\pi\hbar)]\in H^2(P,\mathbb Z)$}

= Pre-quantization
{synonym}

<Prequantization> realizes a <symplectic manifold> using a Hermitian <line bundle> with connection whose curvature is $i\omega/\hbar$ in the convention $\iota_{X_f}\omega=df$. Its existence requires integral symplectic periods. On sections, the <Kostant-Souriau prequantum operator> represents every classical observable and its <Poisson bracket>, but the resulting states depend on the full phase space. A <polarization in geometric quantization> removes this excess of variables.

= Kostant-Souriau prequantum operator
{c}
{parent=Prequantization}
{title2=$\widehat f=-i\hbar\nabla_{X_f}+f$}

= Prequantum operator
{synonym}

For $\iota_{X_f}\omega=df$ and $F_\nabla=i\omega/\hbar$, the curvature <commutator> and $[X_f,X_g]=-X_{\{f,g\}}$ give $[\widehat f,\widehat g]=i\hbar\widehat{\{f,g\}}$. In cotangent coordinates, $\theta=p\,dq$ gives $\widehat f=-i\hbar X_f+f-\theta(X_f)$. Restriction to a vertical polarization yields $\widehat q=q$ and $\widehat p=-i\hbar\partial_q$.

= Prequantum line bundle
{parent=Prequantization}
{title2=$F_\nabla=i\omega/\hbar$}

A <prequantum line bundle> is a Hermitian <line bundle> over a <symplectic manifold> with unitary <connection on a vector bundle> of the displayed curvature. With $\omega=-d\theta$, a local connection is $\nabla=d-i\theta/\hbar$. Reversing the Hamiltonian-vector-field convention or passing to the dual <line bundle> reverses the corresponding curvature convention. Integrality of $\omega/(2\pi\hbar)$ on closed two-cycles is the topological existence condition.

= Hamiltonian group action
{c}
{parent=Symplectic geometry}

= Hamiltonian action
{c}
{synonym}

A Hamiltonian group action is a <Lie group action> on a <symplectic manifold> with an equivariant <moment map> $\mu:M\to\mathfrak g^*$. In the sign convention $\iota_{X_H}\omega=dH$, each component satisfies $d\langle\mu,X\rangle=\iota_{X_M}\omega$, where $X_M$ is the <fundamental vector field>. The opposite <Hamiltonian vector field> convention reverses the sign.

= Weakly Hamiltonian action
{parent=Hamiltonian group action}

A symplectic <Lie group action> is weakly Hamiltonian when every <fundamental vector field> is a <Hamiltonian vector field>. A <Hamiltonian group action> additionally supplies Hamiltonians as components of an equivariant <moment map>. Constants in the component Hamiltonians do not affect their <vector fields>, but the ability to choose them with the required equivariance is an additional global condition.

= Hamiltonian existence for a semisimple symplectic action
{parent=Weakly Hamiltonian action}
{c}
{title2=$[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]=\mathfrak g$}

For a symplectic left <Lie group action>, use actual fundamental fields $\xi_P$ and $\iota_{X_f}\omega=df$. They satisfy $[\xi_P,\eta_P]=-[\xi,\eta]_P$. <Cartan's magic formula> then gives $\iota_{[\xi,\eta]_P}\omega=d[\omega(\xi_P,\eta_P)]$. Thus every commutator generator has a globally exact contraction. A <semisimple Lie algebra> is perfect, so every generator has a global Hamiltonian. The separate <semisimple moment-map equivariance> result removes the constant two-cocycle obstruction and gives an equivariant <moment map> for a connected group.

= Symplectic reduction
{parent=Hamiltonian group action}
{title2=$\mu^{-1}(c)/G$}
{wiki}

For a <Hamiltonian group action>, a <regular value> level preserved by a free proper <group action> has a smooth quotient. The restricted <symplectic form> descends to a <symplectic form> on that quotient when the kernel is exactly the orbit directions; this is symplectic reduction.

= Lagrangian lift through circle reduction
{parent=Symplectic reduction}
{title2=$\widetilde L=\pi^{-1}(L)$}

At a regular free <circle group> level of a <moment map>, the inverse image of a <Lagrangian submanifold> of the reduced space is a <Lagrangian submanifold> of the original space. Its restricted <symplectic form> is the pullback of the reduced form and hence vanishes. Its dimension is one more than that of the reduced Lagrangian, exactly half the ambient dimension. It is a <circle bundle> over the original Lagrangian.

= Planar rotational symplectic reduction
{parent=Symplectic reduction}
{title2=$\omega_{\mathrm{red}}=dr\wedge dp_r$}

For a planar <cotangent bundle>, rotation has <moment map> $\ell=xp_y-yp_x$. At $\ell\ne0$ its level is regular and the circle action is free. Polar canonical coordinates give $\omega=dr\wedge dp_r+d\theta\wedge d\ell$. Fixing $\ell$ and quotienting $\theta$ leaves $T^*\mathbb R_{>0}$ with the displayed form. The unit-mass free <Hamiltonian> becomes $p_r^2/2+\ell^2/(2r^2)$.

= Symplectic reduction of an isotropic oscillator
{parent=Symplectic reduction}
{title2=$H^{-1}(E)/S^1\cong\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$}

For $H=\sum_j(q_j^2+p_j^2)/2$ and $E>0$, the oscillator flow is a free circle action on the sphere of radius $\sqrt{2E}$. Its quotient is <complex projective space>. For two modes, the reduced form in $w=z_2/z_1$ is $iE\,dw\wedge d\bar w/(1+|w|^2)^2$, of total area $2\pi E$. Reduction at zero energy is singular instead of a smooth quotient of the asserted dimension.

= Marsden-Weinstein theorem
{c}
{parent=Symplectic reduction}
{title2=$\mu^{-1}(c)/G$}

For a <Hamiltonian group action>, let $c$ be a <regular value> of the <moment map> fixed by the <coadjoint action>, and assume the action on $\mu^{-1}(c)$ is free and proper. The quotient is a <symplectic manifold> of dimension $\dim M-2\dim G$. Its unique reduced <symplectic form> is characterized by $\pi^*\omega_c=\iota^*\omega$, where $\iota$ includes the level set and $\pi$ is the quotient projection. The kernel of the restricted two-form consists precisely of the orbit directions. More general noncentral levels use their stabilizer group; the stated central-level version suffices for <circle group> reduction.

= Restriction of a Hamiltonian group action
{parent=Hamiltonian group action}

Restriction to a <Lie subgroup> with <Lie algebra> inclusion $i:\mathfrak h\to\mathfrak g$ has <moment map> $i^*\mu$. The <dual map> $i^*$ composes with the original <moment map>.

= Moment map
{parent=Hamiltonian group action}
{title2=$\mu:M\to\mathfrak g^*$}
{wiki}

= Momentum map
{synonym}

A moment map assigns to each element of a <Lie algebra> a <Hamiltonian function> for its <fundamental vector field>, and is equivariant for the <coadjoint action>. For an abelian <Lie group>, constant shifts are allowed; the numerical scale also depends on the chosen <Lie algebra> generators and the normalization of the <symplectic form>.

= Unitary moment map of an isotropic oscillator
{parent=Moment map}
{title2=$\mu_A=z^\dagger A z,\quad z=(x+ip)/\sqrt2$}

On the canonical <cotangent bundle> $T^*\mathbb R^n$, $\omega=i\sum dz_j\wedge d\bar z_j$ and $H=z^\dagger z$. The <unitary group> acts by $z\mapsto Uz$, preserving both. For a Hermitian $A$, the generator $-iA$ has Hamiltonian $\mu_A=z^\dagger Az$. The <Poisson bracket> satisfies $\{\mu_A,\mu_B\}=\mu_{-i[A,B]}$, with no central term. The central generator has moment map $H$; imaginary antisymmetric matrices give ordinary <angular momentum>, while real symmetric matrices give quadratic observables mixing position and momentum.

= Moment-map equivariance obstruction
{parent=Moment map}
{title2=$\kappa(X,Y)=\{\mu_X,\mu_Y\}-\mu_{[X,Y]}$}

For a weakly <Hamiltonian> left <Lie group action> on a connected <symplectic manifold>, choose $\iota_{X_f}\omega=df$. <Fundamental vector fields> and <Hamiltonian vector fields> are both antihomomorphisms, so the displayed difference is constant. The <Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket> makes it a two-cocycle. Changing $\mu_X$ by $b(X)$ changes the difference by $-b([X,Y])$. Thus its class in <Lie algebra cohomology> obstructs choosing an equivariant <moment map>. For a connected group, vanishing of this class gives infinitesimal and group equivariance.

= Fixed-point normalization removes the moment-map cocycle
{parent=Moment-map equivariance obstruction}
{title2=$\{\mu_\xi,\mu_\eta\}=\mu_{[\xi,\eta]}$}

Suppose a symplectic <Lie group action> on a connected <symplectic manifold> has global component Hamiltonians and a common fixed point $p_0$. Choose their constants linearly so $\mu_\xi(p_0)=0$. The difference $c(\xi,\eta)=\{\mu_\xi,\mu_\eta\}-\mu_{[\xi,\eta]}$ has zero <Hamiltonian vector field> because fundamental fields and Hamiltonian fields have compatible Lie-bracket conventions; it is therefore constant. At $p_0$, all fundamental fields vanish, so both terms in $c$ vanish. Thus $c=0$ everywhere and the <Poisson brackets> of the components reproduce the group <Lie algebra>. For a connected group this also yields coadjoint equivariance. A linear unitary action on a vector space has the origin as such a fixed point.

= Semisimple moment-map equivariance
{parent=Moment-map equivariance obstruction}
{title2=$H^2(\mathfrak g,\mathbb R)=0$}

For a semisimple <Lie algebra>, its nondegenerate <Killing form> turns a two-cocycle into an operator $D$ through $B(DX,Y)=\kappa(X,Y)$. The cocycle identity makes $D$ a derivation. Choose $Z$ by $B(Z,X)=\operatorname{tr}(D\operatorname{ad}X)$. Using $[D,\operatorname{ad}X]=\operatorname{ad}(DX)$ and cyclicity of trace gives $B([Z,X],Y)=B(DX,Y)$, so $D=\operatorname{ad}Z$. Consequently $\kappa(X,Y)=B(Z,[X,Y])$ is a coboundary. This proves that component Hamiltonians can be normalized to a <Lie algebra> homomorphism.

= Moment map for rotation of the complex projective line
{parent=Moment map}
{title2=$H(z)=|z|^2/(1+|z|^2)$}

For the <symplectic form> $\omega=i\,dz\wedge d\bar z/(1+|z|^2)^2$, the <circle group> rotates $z$ by $e^{i\theta}$. Its real generator is $K=iz\partial_z-i\bar z\partial_{\bar z}$. The contraction is $\iota_K\omega=-dH$, so $H$ is a <Hamiltonian function> in this sign convention. In the other chart $w=1/z$, $H=1/(1+|w|^2)$, proving smoothness at infinity. Its image is $[0,1]$ with the endpoints at the fixed poles. The <symplectic area> is $2\pi$; rescaling to the round area $4\pi$ would double this moment function.

= Anti-symplectic map
{parent=Symplectic geometry}

= Anti-symplectic
{synonym}

An anti-symplectic map between <symplectic manifolds> is a <diffeomorphism> $f$ satisfying $f^*\omega'=-\omega$. For <symplectic vector spaces>, the analogous definition uses an invertible <linear map>.

= Anti-symplectic involution
{parent=Anti-symplectic map}
{title2=$\sigma^2=I,\ \sigma^*\omega=-\omega$}

An anti-symplectic involution is an <anti-symplectic map> whose square is the identity. Its nonempty <fixed-point set> is a <Lagrangian submanifold>; the linear version has complementary $+1$ and $-1$ <Lagrangian subspaces>.

= Lagrangian fixed locus of an anti-symplectic involution
{c}
{parent=Anti-symplectic involution}

= Lagrangian fixed loci of anti-symplectic involutions
{c}
{synonym}

A nonempty <fixed-point set> of an <anti-symplectic involution> is a <Lagrangian submanifold>. Local linearization identifies its <tangent space> with the $+1$ <eigenspace> of the derivative.

= Symplectic group
{title2=$Sp(2n,\mathbb R)$}
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{wiki}

The real symplectic group consists of matrices $A$ satisfying $A^TJA=J$. It is an embedded matrix Lie group of dimension $2n^2+n$ in size $2n$.

= Symplectic group over a field
{parent=Symplectic group}
{title2=$Sp(V,B)$}

For a finite-dimensional <vector space> over any <field> with a <nondegenerate> <alternating bilinear form> $B$, the <symplectic group over a field> consists of <invertible linear maps> preserving $B$. In a <symplectic basis> its matrices satisfy $g^{\mathsf T}Jg=J$. Matching two <symplectic bases> gives a unique such <linear map>, and a <symplectic basis> exists by successively splitting off paired vectors. This includes arbitrary-characteristic and infinite-field versions, beyond the real <Lie group> and the <symplectic group over a finite field>.

= Symplectic matrix
{parent=Symplectic group}
{title2=$A^TJA=J$}

An invertible real matrix preserving the <standard symplectic form>. With $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\-I&0\end{pmatrix}$, it satisfies $A^TJA=J$, equivalently $AJA^T=J$. The first identity describes preservation of the form; the second describes preservation of coordinate <Poisson brackets>.

= Symplectic parabolic subgroup
{parent=Symplectic group}

The stabilizer of a totally isotropic $k$-space in a finite symplectic space also stabilizes its orthogonal complement. Its block decomposition has a unipotent normal subgroup $Q$ and a block-diagonal complement $L\cong\operatorname{GL}_k(q)\times\operatorname{Sp}_{2m-2k}(q)$. The paired $k$-space carries the contragredient action. A row-vector convention gives upper-triangular blocks; a column-vector convention transposes the arrangement. The <unipotent radical count for a symplectic parabolic subgroup> yields its order.

= Symplectic Lagrangian stabilizer
{parent=Symplectic parabolic subgroup}
{title2=$P_m=\operatorname{Sym}_m(q)\rtimes GL_m(q)$}

With a <Lagrangian subspace> first in a paired <symplectic basis>, its stabilizer consists of $\begin{pmatrix}A&AS\\0&A^{-\mathsf T}\end{pmatrix}$ with $S$ symmetric. The additive symmetric-matrix <unipotent radical> has order $q^{m(m+1)/2}$; the <Levi subgroup> is $GL_m(q)$, acting by $S\mapsto ASA^{\mathsf T}$. Its index in $Sp_{2m}(q)$ is $\prod_{i=1}^m(q^i+1)$, the number of Lagrangian subspaces.

= Symplectic point stabilizer
{parent=Symplectic parabolic subgroup}
{title2=$P_1=U\rtimes(\mathbb F_q^\times\times Sp_{2m-2}(q))$}

For a line $\langle e\rangle$, complete $(e,f)$ to a <symplectic basis> with complementary symplectic space $W$. The <unipotent radical> has elements $u(b,t)$ sending $e$ to $e$, $w$ to $w-B(w,b)e$ and $f$ to $f+b+te$. Its multiplication is $(b,t)(b',t')=(b+b',t+t'+B(b,b'))$, giving order $q^{2m-1}$. Its commutator is central with parameter $2B(b,b')$. It is Heisenberg-type in odd characteristic and abelian in characteristic two. The full stabilizer also scales $e,f$ inversely and acts symplectically on $W$.

= Unipotent radical count for a symplectic parabolic subgroup
{parent=Symplectic parabolic subgroup}
{title2=$|Q|=q^{2k(m-k)+k(k+1)/2}$}

In row-block order $(W',U,W)$ with dual bases, a unit-diagonal symplectic flag stabilizer satisfies $E=J_UD^T$ and $F-F^T=DJ_UD^T$. The matrix $D$ is arbitrary. The latter right side is alternating, so each off-diagonal pair of entries of $F$ gives one free choice and each diagonal entry is free. Consequently
$$
|Q|=q^{2k(m-k)+k(k+1)/2}.
$$
Reversed dual-basis order inserts a reversal matrix but does not change the count. The argument holds in characteristic two without division by two.

= Finite symplectic group order
{parent=Symplectic group}
{title2=$q^{m^2}\prod_{i=1}^m(q^{2i}-1)$}

Counting successive symplectic pairs gives
$$
|\operatorname{Sp}_{2m}(q)|=q^{m^2}\prod_{i=1}^m(q^{2i}-1).
$$
For each nonzero first vector there are $q^{2m-1}$ partners with pairing one, after which its nondegenerate plane splits off. The exact defining-characteristic part is $q^{m^2}$, realized by an upper symplectic unitriangular subgroup.

= Symplectic group as a regular level set
{parent=Symplectic group}

The map $F:M_{2n}(\mathbb R)\to\operatorname{Skew}_{2n}(\mathbb R)$ given by $F(A)=A^TJA$ has $Sp(2n,\mathbb R)=F^{-1}(J)$. At a symplectic $A$,
$$
DF_A(H)=H^TJA+A^TJH
$$
is surjective, so the <regular level set theorem> makes the symplectic group an embedded submanifold.

= Tangent space of the symplectic group
{parent=Symplectic group}

At the identity, the symplectic Lie algebra consists of $X$ satisfying $X^TJ+JX=0$; tangent spaces elsewhere are its left translates.

= Poisson manifold
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{wiki}

A Poisson manifold is a smooth manifold with a bilinear bracket on smooth functions that is antisymmetric, obeys the <Jacobi identity>, and is a derivation in each argument. Its Poisson bivector may have nonconstant rank, unlike the inverse of a symplectic form.

= Momentum-space Poisson structure for a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{title2=$\pi^{ij}=e\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$}

A uniform <magnetic field> defines a constant <Poisson bivector> on momentum space by the displayed components. For the Hamiltonian $E(\mathbf p)$ and convention $\dot f=\{f,E\}$, it gives $\dot{\mathbf p}=e\nabla E\times\mathbf B$. Constancy makes every term in the coordinate <Jacobi identity> vanish. If $e\mathbf B\ne0$, the <symplectic leaves> are the planes $\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf p=c$, and energy is conserved along the flow. The whole three-dimensional momentum space is not symplectic, and an energy surface need not itself be a Poisson submanifold.

= Symplectic foliation
{parent=Poisson manifold}

The <symplectic foliation> of a <Poisson manifold> consists of maximal connected integral manifolds of its Hamiltonian distribution. Each leaf has a nondegenerate closed two-form inverse to the restricted Poisson structure, with compatible sign conventions. Ranks can differ between leaves, making this a singular foliation. Hamiltonian flows remain within a leaf, and <Casimir functions of a Poisson manifold> are constant along every leaf; a Casimir level set need not be a single leaf.

= Poisson map
{c}
{parent=Poisson manifold}

A smooth map $r:M\to N$ between <Poisson manifolds> is a <Poisson map> when $\{f\circ r,g\circ r\}_M=\{f,g\}_N\circ r$ for all smooth functions on $N$. When a Lie group acts by Poisson automorphisms and the quotient is smooth, brackets of invariant functions descend and make the quotient projection Poisson. The quotient can be Poisson without being symplectic.

= Poisson tensor
{c}
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{title2=$\pi$}

A <Poisson tensor> is a smooth bivector field defining $\{f,g\}=\pi(df,dg)$ whose bracket obeys the Jacobi identity. In coordinates, $\pi^{i\ell}\partial_\ell\pi^{jk}+\pi^{j\ell}\partial_\ell\pi^{ki}+\pi^{k\ell}\partial_\ell\pi^{ij}=0$. Its Hamiltonian distribution integrates into <symplectic leaves>, even though its rank can vary across the manifold. The tensor specifies a bracket without requiring a globally nondegenerate symplectic form.

= Poisson structure
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{c}
{title2=$\{f,g\}=\omega^{ab}f_ag_b$}

A <Poisson structure> is an antisymmetric smooth bivector field whose induced function bracket satisfies the <Jacobi identity>. In coordinates its condition is the vanishing cyclic sum of $\omega^{dc}\partial_d\omega^{ab}$. Applying Jacobi to coordinate functions proves necessity; expanding arbitrary brackets cancels the second derivatives and proves sufficiency. A function bracket-commuting with every function is a <Casimir function of a Poisson manifold>.

= Poisson operator
{parent=Poisson structure}
{c}
{title2=$\{F,G\}=\int\frac{\delta F}{\delta u}\,J\!\left(\frac{\delta G}{\delta u}\right)dx$}

For field <functionals>, a Poisson operator $J$ is an operator whose displayed pairing of <variational derivatives> defines a <Poisson bracket>. It must be skew under the integral pairing and satisfy the <functional> <Jacobi identity>. A constant skew <differential operator>, such as $J=\partial_x$ with periodic or vanishing <boundary conditions>, gives the simplest examples. The <Hamiltonian> evolution is $u_t=J(\delta H/\delta u)$.

= Poisson pencil
{parent=Poisson structure}
{c}
{title2=$\Pi_\lambda=\Pi_1+\lambda\Pi_2$}

Two <Poisson structures> are compatible when every linear combination $\Pi_1+\lambda\Pi_2$ is a <Poisson structure>. This family is a Poisson pencil. In terms of the <Schouten-Nijenhuis bracket>, compatibility means $[\Pi_1,\Pi_2]=0$, in addition to the two individual <Jacobi identities>. Constant <Poisson bivectors> are compatible. Over a real <manifold>, complex parameter values refer to the complexification of its <function> algebra.

= Lenard-Magri recursion
{parent=Poisson pencil}
{c}
{title2=$\Pi_1dH_n=-\Pi_2dH_{n-1}$}

Start from a <Casimir function> $H_0$ of the first <Poisson structure>. Whenever possible, choose $H_n$ whose first <Hamiltonian vector field> is minus the second <Hamiltonian vector field> of $H_{n-1}$. A sequence extending indefinitely is the <coefficient> sequence of a formal <Casimir function> for the <Poisson pencil>. Its <functions> pairwise commute for both <Poisson brackets>. Existence at each stage is an additional solvability requirement; compatibility alone does not guarantee it.

= Obstruction to a Lenard-Magri recursion
{parent=Lenard-Magri recursion}

The next step of a <Lenard-Magri recursion> requires $-\Pi_2dH_{n-1}$ to be a <Hamiltonian vector field> for $\Pi_1$. It must be tangent to the <symplectic leaves>, and its contraction with the leaf <symplectic form> must have a global potential, with a compatible smooth extension between leaves. Even the first step may fail: on $\mathbb R^2$, take $\Pi_1=0$, $\Pi_2=\partial_x\wedge\partial_y$ and $H_0=x$. Both <Poisson structures> are compatible, but $\Pi_1dH_1=-\Pi_2dx$ cannot hold, because its left side is zero and its right side is nonzero.

= Commuting coefficients of a Poisson pencil Casimir
{parent=Lenard-Magri recursion}
{title2=$\{H_i,H_j\}_1=\{H_i,H_j\}_2=0$}

For a <Lenard-Magri recursion>, antisymmetry shifts an index: $\{H_i,H_j\}_1=\{H_{i-1},H_{j+1}\}_1$ when $i\geq1$. Repeating reaches $\{H_0,H_{i+j}\}_1=0$, because $H_0$ is a <Casimir function>. The recursion then gives $\{H_i,H_j\}_2=-\{H_{i+1},H_j\}_1=0$. This proof applies to every finite initial portion for the indices that can be shifted inside it, and to all pairs in an infinite sequence.

= Lie-Poisson bracket
{c}
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{title2=$\{F,G\}(\ell)=\ell([dF_\ell,dG_\ell])$}

= Linear Poisson bracket
{synonym}

The <dual space> $\mathfrak g^*$ of a finite-dimensional <Lie algebra> has this canonical <Poisson bracket>. For a basis $v^i$, linear coordinates $x^i(\ell)=\ell(v^i)$ give $\{x^i,x^j\}=c^{ij}_kx^k$. The Lie algebra's <Jacobi identity> is precisely the <coordinate Jacobi condition for a Poisson bivector>. This construction is intrinsic on the dual space; writing the same formula in an arbitrary manifold chart does not by itself provide a compatible global atlas.

= Poisson bivector
{c}
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{title2=$\omega=\frac12\omega^{ij}\partial_i\wedge\partial_j$}

A smooth antisymmetric contravariant two-tensor defines a biderivation $\{F,G\}=\omega(dF,dG)$. It is a Poisson bivector when this bracket satisfies the <Jacobi identity>. Its rank may vary; where it is nondegenerate it inverts to a <symplectic form>. The <coordinate Jacobi condition for a Poisson bivector> makes the integrability constraint explicit.

= Closure of the inverse of a nondegenerate Poisson bivector
{parent=Poisson bivector}
{title2=$d\Omega=0$}

A nondegenerate <Poisson bivector> inverts to a <closed differential form> of degree two. Differentiating $S=P^{-1}$ gives $\partial_dS=-S(\partial_dP)S$. Contracting the <coordinate Jacobi condition for a Poisson bivector> with three copies of $S$ yields $\partial_iS_{jk}+\partial_jS_{ki}+\partial_kS_{ij}=0$, which is precisely closure of the resulting <symplectic form>. An overall sign in the inverse convention does not change closure.

= Coordinate Jacobi condition for a Poisson bivector
{parent=Poisson bivector}
{title2=$\omega^{im}\partial_m\omega^{jk}+\omega^{jm}\partial_m\omega^{ki}+\omega^{km}\partial_m\omega^{ij}=0$}

The <Jacobi identity> for a <Poisson bivector> reduces to the displayed cyclic identity in every local chart. Necessity follows by applying the bracket to coordinate functions. Sufficiency follows because the second-derivative terms in an arbitrary Jacobiator cancel by antisymmetry, leaving this coefficient multiplying first derivatives of the three functions.

= Casimir function of a Poisson manifold
{parent=Poisson manifold}

= Casimir functions of a Poisson manifold
{c}
{synonym}

= Casimir function
{c}
{synonym}

A Casimir function $C$ Poisson-commutes with every smooth function, $\{C,f\}=0$. Its regular level sets are unions of <symplectic leaves>.

= Symplectic leaf
{parent=Poisson manifold}
{wiki}

A symplectic leaf is a maximal connected immersed submanifold on which the Poisson bivector has constant full rank and therefore inverts to a <symplectic form>.

= Rotational Lie-Poisson structure on R3
{title2=$\{x,y\}=z$}
{parent=Poisson manifold}

The rotational Lie-Poisson bracket on $\mathbb R^3$ is
$$
\{x_i,x_j\}=\epsilon_{ijk}x_k.
$$
Its Casimir is $x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2$, and every sphere centered at the origin is a two-dimensional <symplectic leaf> on which $SO(3)$ acts symplectically.

= Two-out-of-three property for unitary structures
{parent=Symplectic geometry}

On $\mathbb R^{2n}$ let $J_0$ be the standard <complex structure>, let $\omega_0$ be the standard <symplectic form>, and let $g_0(u,v)=\omega_0(u,J_0v)$ be the Euclidean <inner product>. A linear map preserving any two of $J_0$, $\omega_0$, and $g_0$ preserves the third. Equivalently,
$$
GL(n,\mathbb C)\cap Sp(2n,\mathbb R)
=Sp(2n,\mathbb R)\cap O(2n)
=O(2n)\cap GL(n,\mathbb C)
=U(n).
$$

= Lagrangian subspace
{c}
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{wiki}

A Lagrangian subspace of a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic vector space> $(V,\omega)$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace $L$ on which $\omega$ vanishes.

= Lagrangian complement
{parent=Lagrangian subspace}
{c}
{title2=$E=L\oplus K$}

A <Lagrangian complement> to $L$ in a <symplectic vector space> is a <Lagrangian subspace> $K$ with $E=L\oplus K$. Such complements exist smoothly for Lagrangian subbundles. Identify any initial complement with $L^*$ using the symplectic cross-pairing. If its internal pairing is $b(\eta,\zeta)$, correct its lift by a vector $a\eta\in L$ with $\zeta(a\eta)=-b(\eta,\zeta)/2$. The corrected complement has zero internal pairing.

= Lagrangian Grassmannian
{title2=$\operatorname{LGr}(V)$}
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian subspace}
{wiki}

The Lagrangian Grassmannian is the space of all <Lagrangian subspaces> of a symplectic vector space. The <unitary group> acts transitively on $\operatorname{LGr}(\mathbb R^{2n})$, and the stabilizer of $\mathbb R^n$ is the <orthogonal group> $O(n)$, giving
$$
\operatorname{LGr}(\mathbb R^{2n})\cong U(n)/O(n).
$$

= Maslov cycle
{parent=Lagrangian Grassmannian}
{c}

Relative to a fixed <Lagrangian subspace> $L_0$, the Maslov cycle consists of Lagrangian subspaces $L$ with $L\cap L_0\ne0$. In a two-dimensional symplectic space all lines are Lagrangian, and the cycle is the single line $L_0$ in the projective line. Fibrewise versions yield sections of a projective-line bundle and record passage through a prescribed vertical direction.

= Vertical Maslov cycle of a surface thermostat
{parent=Maslov cycle}
{title2=$\Lambda_V=\{\mathbb RF(x,v)\oplus\mathbb RV(x,v)\}$}

For a <surface thermostat>, the planes containing $F$ form the <projective bundle> of $T(SM)/\mathbb RF$. The vertical Maslov cycle is the section selecting the image of the <vertical vector field of a surface unit tangent bundle>. It is a smooth hypersurface. In the local projective chart $\mathbb RF+\mathbb R(V+rH)$, it is $r=0$; the lifted flow satisfies $\dot r=1$ on the cycle, hence crosses it transversely.

= Maslov index
{title2=$\mu$}
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian Grassmannian}
{wiki}

The Maslov index assigns an <integer> to a loop of <Lagrangian subspaces>. Under $\operatorname{LGr}(\mathbb R^{2n})\cong U(n)/O(n)$, it is the degree of the map induced by $A\mapsto\det(A)^2$.

= Maslov map
{title2=$\det^2:U(n)/O(n)\to S^1$}
{c}
{parent=Maslov index}

The Maslov map is well defined because every matrix in $O(n)$ has determinant $\pm1$. The loop
$$
t\longmapsto e^{\pi it}\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R^{n-1}
$$
maps to $e^{2\pi it}$ and therefore has Maslov index one.

= Lagrangian Grassmannian bundle
{title2=$\mathcal LGr(E)$}
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian Grassmannian}

For a rank-$2n$ symplectic <vector bundle> $E\to B$, the Lagrangian Grassmannian bundle has fiber $\operatorname{LGr}(E_b)$ over $b$. A symplectic trivialization of $E$ induces a trivialization $\mathcal LGr(E)\cong B\times\operatorname{LGr}(\mathbb R^{2n})$.

= Euler class of the Lagrangian-line bundle of an oriented plane bundle
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian Grassmannian bundle}

If $E\to B$ is an oriented real plane bundle, then $\mathcal LGr(E)=\mathbb P(E)$ is an oriented <circle bundle>. Its transition rotations have twice the angle of those of $E$, so
$$
e(\mathcal LGr(E))=2e(E).
$$

= Symplectic manifold
{parent=Symplectic geometry}
{wiki}

A symplectic manifold is an even-dimensional <smooth manifold> $M$ equipped with a closed <nondegenerate bilinear form>[nondegenerate] differential two-form $\omega$.

= Symplectic vector field
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{title2=$\mathcal L_X\omega=0$}

A <vector field> on a <symplectic manifold> is symplectic when its flow preserves the <symplectic form>. Infinitesimally this is $\mathcal L_X\omega=0$. By <Cartan's magic formula> it is equivalent to $d(\iota_X\omega)=0$. A <Hamiltonian vector field> has an exact contraction with the form, so is symplectic. On the standard two-dimensional <torus>, $\partial_x$ contracts to the closed but nonexact form $dy$, providing a symplectic non-Hamiltonian example.

= Coisotropic submanifold
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

A coisotropic submanifold $N$ satisfies $(T_pN)^\omega\subset T_pN$, where the superscript denotes the <symplectic orthogonal complement>. Every hypersurface of a <symplectic manifold> is coisotropic.

= Characteristic line field
{parent=Coisotropic submanifold}
{title2=$\ker(\omega|_{TN})$}

For a hypersurface $N$ of a <symplectic manifold>, the restriction of the <symplectic form> has a one-dimensional kernel, its characteristic line field. On a <regular value> level $N=H^{-1}(c)$, this line is generated by the <Hamiltonian vector field> of $H$.

= Isotropic submanifold
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

An isotropic submanifold has every <tangent space> an <isotropic subspace of a symplectic vector space>. Its dimension is at most half the ambient dimension, and equality makes it a <Lagrangian submanifold>.

= Symplectic volume
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{title2=$\int_M\omega^n/n!$}

The <symplectic form> on a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold> determines the positive <volume form> $\omega^n/n!$ and its total integral, the symplectic volume, using the induced <orientation>.

= Symplectic volume form
{parent=Symplectic volume}
{title2=$\omega^n/n!$}

= Phase-space volume
{synonym}

= Liouville measure
{c}
{synonym}

The top-degree <differential form> $\omega^n/n!$ on a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold>. Every <canonical transformation> preserves this form. For $n>1$, preservation of this volume alone does not imply preservation of the <symplectic form>.

= Symplectic orientation
{parent=Symplectic volume}
{title2=$\omega^n>0$}

A <symplectic form> on a $2n$-dimensional <smooth manifold> determines the <orientation> in which the nowhere-zero <volume form> $\omega^n/n!$ is positive. Consequently every <symplectic manifold> is orientable. In dimension two this forbids a <symplectic form> on the <Möbius strip>.

= Exact symplectic manifold
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

An exact symplectic manifold has a <symplectic form> that is an <exact differential form>, written here $\omega=-d\lambda$. A positive-dimensional example without boundary cannot be <compact>, by the <generalized Stokes theorem> applied to the top <wedge product of differential forms>.

= Liouville vector field
{parent=Exact symplectic manifold}
{c}
{title2=$\iota_Z\omega=\lambda,\quad\mathcal L_Z\omega=\omega$}

For an <exact symplectic manifold> with $\omega=d\lambda$, the <Liouville vector field> is defined by $\iota_Z\omega=\lambda$. <Cartan's magic formula> gives $\mathcal L_Z\omega=\omega$. On a <cotangent bundle> using $\omega=-d\alpha$, take $\lambda=-\alpha$; then $Z=\sum_i p_i\partial_{p_i}$ generates the <cotangent fiber-dilation flow>.

= Cotangent fiber-dilation flow
{parent=Liouville vector field}
{title2=$D_s(x,p)=(x,e^sp)$}

The vertical <Liouville vector field> on a <cotangent bundle> has complete flow $D_s(x,p)=(x,e^sp)$. Its pullbacks scale both the <Liouville one-form> and the canonical <symplectic form> by $e^s$. As $s\to-\infty$, each orbit tends to its base point on the <zero section>. This contraction proves <rigidity of cotangent Liouville-form preservation>.

= Symplectic cohomology obstruction
{parent=Exact symplectic manifold}
{title2=$[\omega]^n\ne0$}

On a nonempty compact $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold> without boundary, with $n\geq1$, the class $[\omega]^n$ in top-degree <de Rham cohomology> is nonzero, since its integral in the <symplectic orientation> is positive. In particular $\omega$ cannot be an <exact differential form>: if $\omega=d\alpha$, then $\omega^n=d(\alpha\wedge\omega^{n-1})$ and the <generalized Stokes theorem> would make that integral zero. Thus vanishing second <de Rham cohomology> obstructs a closed positive-dimensional <symplectic manifold>.

= Symplectic form
{title2=$\omega$}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A symplectic form is a <closed differential form> of degree two whose value on every tangent space is a nondegenerate alternating bilinear form. In canonical position-momentum coordinates it is $\sum_i dq_i\wedge dp_i$.

= Standard symplectic form
{title2=$\omega_0$}
{parent=Symplectic form}

The standard symplectic form on $\mathbb R^{2n}$ is
$$
\omega_0=\sum_{j=1}^n dx_j\wedge dy_j.
$$

= Symplectic ball
{title2=$B^{2n}(r)$}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A symplectic ball is a Euclidean ball in $(\mathbb R^{2n},\omega_0)$, or its image under a <symplectic embedding>.

= Symplectic embedding
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A symplectic embedding $\iota:(N,\omega_N)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega_M)$ is a <smooth embedding> satisfying $\iota^*\omega_M=\omega_N$.

= Symplectic cylinder
{parent=Symplectic embedding}
{title2=$Z^{2n}(R)=B^2(R)\times\mathbb R^{2n-2}$}

The standard symplectic cylinder restricts one conjugate coordinate pair to a disk and leaves the remaining symplectic coordinates unrestricted. Its normalized <symplectic capacity> is $\pi R^2$, despite its infinite <symplectic volume> when $n>1$.

= Capacity of a bounded set thickened by a symplectic subspace
{parent=Symplectic cylinder}
{title2=$0<c(U+W)<\infty$}

If $U$ is nonempty, bounded and open in standard symplectic Euclidean space, and $W$ is a codimension-two <symplectic subspace>, a linear symplectic change of coordinates makes $W=\{q_1=p_1=0\}$. Boundedness of the remaining coordinate projection places $U+W$ inside a finite-radius <symplectic cylinder>. Openness puts a small ball inside it. Monotonicity and normalization of a <symplectic capacity> give the displayed strict bounds.

= Symplectic blowup
{title2=$\widetilde M$}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A symplectic blowup at a point removes a small <symplectic ball> and collapses its Hopf-circle boundary fibers, replacing the center by an <exceptional divisor> $E\cong\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$. The resulting form agrees with the original form away from the surgery region, and its integral over a projective line in $E$ records the blowup size. In real dimension four, $E\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$ and $E^2=-1$.

= First Chern class formula for a symplectic blowup
{parent=Symplectic blowup}
{title2=$c_1(T\widetilde X)=\pi^*c_1(TX)-(n-1)\operatorname{PD}[E]$}

For a <symplectic blowup> of a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold>, the displayed formula gives its <first Chern class>. The exceptional divisor has <normal bundle> $\mathcal O(-1)$; the discrepancy coefficient is $n-1$. In dimension four it is one.

= Symplectic blowup size changes volume
{parent=Symplectic blowup}
{title2=$\operatorname{Vol}(\widetilde X)=\operatorname{Vol}(X)-\varepsilon^n/n!$}

For a closed $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold>, a <symplectic blowup> of size $\varepsilon=\pi R^2$ removes the <symplectic volume> of the radius-$R$ ball. Its exceptional divisor has lower dimension and contributes no ambient volume. The displayed volume loss shows that different size choices can give forms that are not related by a <symplectomorphism>.

= Darboux theorem
{disambiguate=symplectic geometry}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

= Symplectic Darboux theorem
{c}
{synonym}

The Darboux theorem says that every point of a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold> has local coordinates in which the <symplectic form> is $\sum_jdx_j\wedge dy_j$. Thus symplectic manifolds have no local invariants beyond dimension.

= Darboux chart
{c}
{parent=Darboux theorem (symplectic geometry)}

= Darboux coordinates
{c}
{synonym}

A Darboux chart is a coordinate chart in which a <symplectic form> becomes the <standard symplectic form>.

= Lefschetz pencil
{title2=$f:X\dashrightarrow\mathbb{CP}^1$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

A Lefschetz pencil on an oriented four-manifold is a map $f:X\setminus B\to\mathbb{CP}^1$ with a finite base locus $B$, locally modeled by $[z_1:z_2]$ at each base point, and isolated critical points locally modeled by $z_1^2+z_2^2$. Blowing up the base points turns the pencil into a <Lefschetz fibration>.

= Odd-genus pencils from a four-divisible polarization
{parent=Lefschetz pencil}
{title2=$g=1+\tfrac12(m^2H^2+mK_X\cdot H),\quad4\mid m$}

On a <smooth projective surface>, fix an <ample divisor> $H$. For sufficiently large $m$, a general pencil in $|mH|$ is a <Lefschetz pencil>. The <adjunction formula> gives the displayed genus. If $m$ is divisible by four, both terms added to $1$ are even integers, so the genus is odd.

= Base-point count for a Lefschetz pencil on a symplectic four-torus
{parent=Lefschetz pencil}
{title2=$b=2g-2$}

The standard four-<torus> has trivial complex tangent bundle, so its <symplectic canonical class> is zero. The <symplectic adjunction formula> gives $F^2=2g-2$ for a smooth genus-$g$ fiber of a symplectic <Lefschetz pencil>. Two generic fibers meet once positively at each base point and nowhere else, giving the displayed count.

= Lefschetz fibration
{c}
{parent=Lefschetz pencil}
{wiki}

A Lefschetz fibration is a map to a surface whose critical points have the complex Morse local model $z_1^2+\cdots+z_n^2$. Away from the critical values it is a smooth <fiber bundle>.

= Euler characteristic of a Lefschetz pencil
{c}
{parent=Lefschetz pencil}

If a Lefschetz pencil on a closed four-manifold $X$ has smooth fiber of genus $g$, $b$ base points and $m$ critical points, then
$$
\chi(X)=4-4g+m-b.
$$
Indeed, blowing up the base points gives $\chi(\widetilde X)=\chi(X)+b$, while a genus-$g$ fibration over $S^2$ contributes $2(2-2g)$ and each Lefschetz critical point contributes one.

= Genus of a symplectic Lefschetz pencil on the complex projective plane
{c}
{parent=Lefschetz pencil}

Every symplectic smooth fiber in a Lefschetz pencil on $\mathbb{CP}^2$ represents $dH$ for an integer $d\geq1$. The <symplectic adjunction formula> gives
$$
g=\frac{(d-1)(d-2)}2.
$$
Conversely, generic pencils of degree-$d$ complex plane curves realize every value in this list.

= Symplectic surface
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A symplectic surface is a two-dimensional <symplectic manifold>. Every embedded curve in it is a <Lagrangian submanifold>.

= Symplectic area
{parent=Symplectic surface}
{title2=$\int_\Sigma\omega$}

The symplectic area of a <compact> <symplectic surface> $(\Sigma,\omega)$ is $\int_\Sigma\omega$, using the <orientation> determined by the <symplectic form>.

= Classification of compact symplectic surfaces
{parent=Symplectic area}

Two <compact> <connected> <symplectic surfaces> are <symplectomorphic> exactly when their <genus of a surface> and <symplectic area> agree. The sufficiency follows from <Moser theorem> applied to a positive interpolation of their <symplectic forms> after an <orientation>-preserving <diffeomorphism>.

= Complementary areas obstruct symplectic equivalence of separating curves
{parent=Classification of compact symplectic surfaces}

A <symplectomorphism> of a closed <symplectic surface> carries the complementary components of a separating simple closed curve to those of its image, preserving their unordered <symplectic areas>. On the unit <sphere> a latitude at height $h$ has complementary areas $2\pi(1-h)$ and $2\pi(1+h)$. Thus the equator cannot be sent to any nonequatorial latitude.

= Symplectomorphism
{title2=$\operatorname{Symp}(M,\omega)$}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

= Symplectomorphic
{synonym}

A symplectomorphism $\phi:(M,\omega)\to(M',\omega')$ is a <diffeomorphism> satisfying $\phi^*\omega'=\omega$.

= Group of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms
{parent=Symplectomorphism}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ham}(M,\omega)$}

= Hamiltonian diffeomorphism group
{c}
{synonym}

This <group> consists of time-one maps of <Hamiltonian isotopies> whose flows are defined throughout the specified time interval. With $\iota_{X_H}\omega=-dH$, composition of isotopies generated by $H_t,K_t$ has generator $H_t+K_t\circ\phi_t^{-1}$, where $\phi_t$ is the first isotopy. The inverse path has generator $-H_t\circ\phi_t$. These formulas prove closure under composition and inversion. On a closed <symplectic manifold>, every smooth time-dependent Hamiltonian has a complete flow over a bounded time interval.

= Compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism group
{parent=Group of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ham}_c(M,\omega)$}

This subgroup uses Hamiltonians whose support in $[0,1]\times M$ is compact. Their flows are the identity outside a fixed compact set. On closed manifolds this support condition imposes no extra restriction. On an open <symplectic manifold>, it excludes nontrivial whole-fiber <cotangent fiber translations>, even when their generating one-form is exact.

= Hofer metric
{parent=Compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism group}
{c}
{title2=$d_H(\phi,\psi)=\inf_{\phi_H^1=\phi^{-1}\psi}\int_0^1(\max H_t-\min H_t)\,dt$}

= Hofer's metric
{c}
{synonym}

The displayed distance uses the oscillation length of a compactly supported <Hamiltonian isotopy>. On a closed manifold one may normalize each Hamiltonian to have zero mean. Additive time-dependent constants do not change the flow or its oscillation. Pullback by a <symplectomorphism> preserves extrema, so this distance is bi-invariant. Nondegeneracy requires an additional geometric argument; it does not follow merely from the definition as an infimum of path lengths.

= Hofer nondegeneracy from rational Lagrangian displacement
{parent=Hofer metric}
{c}

Assume the lower bound $e(L)\geq\gamma(L)/2$ for a closed <rational Lagrangian submanifold>, with matching energy convention. A nonidentity Hamiltonian map displaces a sufficiently small ball about some moved point. Inside that ball place a <Lagrangian torus> formed from equal-area circles. Its <least positive Liouville period> is positive, so the map's norm is at least half this period. This proves positivity of the distance between different maps without appealing to nondegeneracy itself.

= Displacement energy
{disambiguate=symplectic geometry}
{parent=Hofer metric}
{title2=$e(A)=\inf_{\phi(A)\cap A=\varnothing}d_H(\mathrm{id},\phi)$}

= Hamiltonian displacement energy
{c}
{synonym}

The displacement energy of a subset of a <symplectic manifold> is the infimum of the energy norms of compactly supported <Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms> moving it off itself. The infimum is $+\infty$ if no such map exists. One must use the same chosen norm convention in both this definition and any energy lower bound. A compact set can have zero displacement energy even when every nonidentity map has positive norm.

= Zero displacement energy for a compact set in a hyperplane
{parent=Displacement energy (symplectic geometry)}
{title2=$e(A)=0\quad(A\subset\{p_n=0\}\text{ compact})$}

Use the <standard symplectic form> $\sum_jdq_j\wedge dp_j$ and convention $\iota_{X_H}\omega=-dH$. The Hamiltonian $H=\delta q_n$ translates $p_n$ by $\delta$. A fixed compactly supported cutoff equal to one near the set swept for $|\delta|\leq1$ leaves that translation unchanged on the original compact set. Its supremum norm is at most $C|\delta|$, so the translation displaces the set at arbitrarily small cost. This proves zero <Hamiltonian displacement energy> for either Hofer normalization.

= Hofer metric from the spatial supremum norm
{parent=Hofer metric}
{c}
{title2=$\rho_\infty(\phi,\psi)=\inf_{\phi_H^1=\phi^{-1}\psi}\int_0^1\|H_t\|_\infty\,dt$}

Use compactly supported Hamiltonians on open manifolds and zero-mean Hamiltonians on closed manifolds. Because their values straddle zero, $\|H_t\|_\infty\leq\max H_t-\min H_t\leq2\|H_t\|_\infty$. Thus $\rho_\infty\leq d_H\leq2\rho_\infty$. These are two equivalent, but not identically normalized, conventions for <Hofer's metric>. A statement about zero distance or zero displacement energy is independent of this choice.

= Symplectic isotopy
{parent=Symplectomorphism}

A symplectic isotopy is a smooth path $\phi_t$ of <symplectomorphisms> beginning at the identity.

= Strong isotopy of symplectic forms
{parent=Symplectic isotopy}

A strong isotopy of symplectic forms is a smooth path of symplectic forms whose de Rham cohomology class is constant, so that Moser's method can seek an ambient isotopy relating its endpoints.

= Flux homomorphism
{title2=$\operatorname{Flux}$}
{parent=Symplectic isotopy}

For a symplectic isotopy generated by $X_t$, its flux is
$$
\operatorname{Flux}(\phi_t)=\left[\int_0^1\iota_{X_t}\omega\,dt\right]\in H^1(M;\mathbb R).
$$
It vanishes for a <Hamiltonian isotopy>.

= Hamiltonian vector field
{title2=$X_H$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

For a smooth function $H_t:M\to\mathbb R$, the Hamiltonian vector field is defined by $\iota_{X_{H_t}}\omega=-dH_t$, with the sign depending on convention.

= Hamiltonian transitivity on a connected symplectic manifold
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}

Compactly supported <Hamiltonian vector fields> can attain any prescribed tangent vector at a point of a <symplectic manifold>: prescribe the derivative of a local Hamiltonian and multiply by a <bump function>. Composing flows for a tangent <basis> gives an open orbit by the <inverse function theorem>. Every orbit is open, so on a <connected> manifold the compactly supported Hamiltonian transformations act transitively.

= Hamiltonian Lie algebra homomorphism
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}
{title2=$f\mapsto X_f,\quad[X_f,X_g]=X_{\{f,g\}}$}

On a <symplectic manifold>, use $\iota_{X_f}\omega=-df$ and $\{f,g\}=\omega(X_f,X_g)=X_f(g)$ for the <Poisson bracket>. <Cartan's magic formula> gives $\mathcal L_{X_f}\omega=0$. Taking the <interior product of a differential form> with $[X_f,X_g]$ then yields $-d\{f,g\}$, proving the homomorphism. Its kernel consists of locally constant functions, since $X_f=0$ precisely when $df=0$. On a connected manifold these are the constants. The quotient of smooth functions by this kernel is therefore the <Lie algebra> of <Hamiltonian vector fields>. A different contraction sign requires a compatible bracket sign.

= Hamiltonian perturbation preserving one regular level
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}
{c}
{title2=$K=H+\tfrac12\sum_jG_j^2$}

For independent integrals $G_1=H,G_2,\ldots,G_r$, the <Hamiltonian> $K=H+\frac12\sum_jG_j^2$ agrees with $H$ to first order on the common zero level. On a nonempty level $G=c\ne0$, the vector-field difference is $\sum_jc_jX_{G_j}$ and is nowhere zero by independence of the <differentials>. Nonemptiness is necessary if one expresses the conclusion as inequality between restricted <vector fields>.

= Hamiltonian function
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}

A Hamiltonian function is a smooth function whose differential determines a <Hamiltonian vector field>.

= Hamiltonian isotopy
{title2=$\operatorname{Ham}(M,\omega)$}
{c}
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}

= Hamiltonian diffeomorphism
{c}
{synonym}

A Hamiltonian isotopy is the flow of a time-dependent <Hamiltonian vector field>. Its time-one map is a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism.

= Hamiltonian flow preserves the symplectic form
{parent=Hamiltonian vector field}

If $\iota_{X_{H_t}}\omega=-dH_t$ and $\phi_t$ is its flow, then <Cartan's magic formula> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\phi_t^*\omega
=\phi_t^*\mathcal L_{X_{H_t}}\omega
=\phi_t^*d\iota_{X_{H_t}}\omega=0.
$$
Thus every defined time slice of a <Hamiltonian isotopy> is a <symplectomorphism>.

= Compactly supported Hamiltonian translation
{parent=Hamiltonian flow preserves the symplectic form}

Given a bounded set in the symplectic plane and a translation vector $v=(a,b)$, the linear Hamiltonian $H(x,y)=bx-ay$ generates translation by $v$. Multiplying it by a cutoff that equals one on the entire swept region produces a compactly supported <Hamiltonian diffeomorphism> that agrees with the translation on the set.

= Lagrangian submanifold
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

A Lagrangian submanifold of a $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold is an $n$-dimensional submanifold $L$ satisfying $\omega|_L=0$. The symplectic form identifies its <normal bundle> with its <cotangent bundle>.

= Lagrangian graph
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$\operatorname{graph}(\alpha)\subset T^*Q$}

The graph of a <one-form> $\alpha$ on a <smooth manifold> $Q$ is a <Lagrangian submanifold> of its <cotangent bundle> exactly when $d\alpha=0$. Indeed the graph pulls the tautological one-form back to $\alpha$, and therefore pulls the canonical <symplectic form> back to $-d\alpha$. Locally the <Poincaré lemma> writes $\alpha=dS$, giving $p_i=\partial_iS$. Globally a closed one-form may have nonzero periods and no single generating function.

= Lagrangian foliation
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

A foliation of a <symplectic manifold> is Lagrangian when every leaf has half the ambient dimension and the restricted <symplectic form> vanishes. The tangent distribution is a real <polarization in geometric quantization>. Restricted prequantum connections are flat on individual leaves, but nontrivial <holonomy> can obstruct global parallel sections.

= Lagrangian leaf
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian foliation}

A leaf of a <Lagrangian foliation> is an immersed <Lagrangian submanifold> tangent to its integrable distribution. A <prequantum line bundle> restricts to a flat connection along such a leaf, because its curvature is proportional to the <symplectic form>. Nonzero global parallel sections require trivial <holonomy> along every leaf loop.

= Special Lagrangian submanifold
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$\omega|_\Sigma=0,\quad\operatorname{Im}(e^{-i\theta}\Omega)|_\Sigma=0$}

A Lagrangian submanifold in a Calabi–Yau geometry is special when the phase of its holomorphic volume form is constant. With orientation chosen so that the real part is positive, it is a <calibrated submanifold> for $\operatorname{Re}(e^{-i\theta}\Omega)$. In $\mathbb C^3$, its spatial three-fold can describe a supersymmetric curved <D3-brane>.

= Special Lagrangian graph equation
{parent=Special Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$\Delta f=\det D^2f\quad\text{in dimension three at phase zero}$}

For a graph $z=x+i\nabla f$ in $\mathbb C^3$, symmetry of the <Hessian matrix> makes the graph Lagrangian. Pulling back $\Omega=dz^1\wedge dz^2\wedge dz^3$ gives $\det(I+iD^2f)$. Its imaginary part is $\operatorname{tr}D^2f-\det D^2f$, while its real part is $1-\sigma_2(D^2f)$. Vanishing of the imaginary part and positivity of the real part give the displayed equation and oriented branch. The equation is nonlinear even though its linearization near a flat graph is the <Laplace equation>.

// Target: string-theory.bigb

= Thermodynamic potential as a Lagrangian graph
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$L_M=\operatorname{graph}(dM)$}

For a smooth function $M(q)$, its differential defines a <Lagrangian submanifold> of the <cotangent bundle>: pullback of the <canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle> is $dM$, so pullback of the <symplectic form> $-d\theta$ is zero. The graph has half the ambient dimension. With $q=(S,V,N)$ and momentum coordinates $(T,-P,\mu)$, the <first law of thermodynamics> identifies the equilibrium graph with $dM$. This geometric statement does not require extensivity or thermodynamic stability.

= Lagrangian torus
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{c}
{title2=$L\cong\mathbb T^n,\quad\omega|_L=0$}

= Lagrangian tori
{c}
{synonym}

A <Lagrangian submanifold> diffeomorphic to an $n$-dimensional <flat torus> in a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold>. Compact connected regular levels of a <completely integrable Hamiltonian system> are examples, by the <Arnold-Liouville theorem>.

= Lagrangian path-area functional
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$\mathcal A(\gamma)=-\int u^*\omega$}

Choose a reference path between <Lagrangian submanifolds> $L_0,L_1$ in a <symplectic manifold>. With a connecting homotopy strip oriented by $ds\wedge dt$, define $\mathcal A(\gamma)=-\int u^*\omega$. If the <path space> is connected and $H_2(M,L_0\cup L_1)=0$, <relative symplectic-area independence> makes this single-valued up to a global constant. Its derivative is $d\mathcal A(\xi)=\int\omega(\dot\gamma,\xi)dt$, so its critical points are the constant paths in $L_0\cap L_1$. For a <compatible almost complex structure>, its formal downward $L^2$ flow is the <J-holomorphic curve> equation $u_s+Ju_t=0$.

= Relative symplectic-area independence
{parent=Lagrangian path-area functional}

Two homotopy strips with the same endpoint paths and boundary on <Lagrangian submanifolds> glue to a relative $2$-cycle. If that cycle is zero in $H_2(M,L_0\cup L_1)$, its <symplectic area> vanishes: apply <Stokes theorem>, using $d\omega=0$ and the zero restriction of $\omega$ to each Lagrangian. Thus relative homology controls the ambiguity in a <Lagrangian path-area functional>.

= Lagrangian embedding
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

A Lagrangian embedding is a <smooth embedding> whose image is a <Lagrangian submanifold>.

= Lagrangian immersion
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

A Lagrangian immersion is an <immersion> $\iota:L^n\to(M^{2n},\omega)$ satisfying $\iota^*\omega=0$.

= Product Lagrangian immersion
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian immersion}

If $\gamma_i:L_i\to(M_i,\omega_i)$ are one-dimensional immersed curves in symplectic surfaces, then
$$
\gamma_1\times\gamma_2:L_1\times L_2\longrightarrow(M_1\times M_2,\omega_1\oplus\omega_2)
$$
is a <Lagrangian immersion>. A double point of one factor times the other curve becomes a clean self-intersection circle.

= Clean Lagrangian intersection
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian immersion}

A clean Lagrangian intersection is a <clean intersection> between two local Lagrangian sheets. A small exact perturbation of one sheet near a compact clean component replaces the intersection by the critical points of a <Morse function> on that component.

= Lagrangian surgery
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

Lagrangian surgery replaces two transverse local Lagrangian sheets by a smooth Lagrangian neck. For the standard planes $\mathbb R^n$ and $i\mathbb R^n$ in $\mathbb C^n$, a neck is obtained by following a curve between the two coordinate rays and multiplying it by points of $S^{n-1}$. In dimension four, resolving a double point attaches a one-handle to the source surface and lowers its <Euler characteristic> by two.

= Lagrangian connected sum
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian surgery}

If two embedded Lagrangian surfaces meet transversely in one point, <Lagrangian surgery> at that point produces their connected sum. For orientable surfaces $\Sigma_{g_1}$ and $\Sigma_{g_2}$, the result is $\Sigma_{g_1+g_2}$.

= Clean-circle resolution of a Lagrangian surface immersion
{parent=Lagrangian surgery}

Perturb a clean self-intersection circle of a <Lagrangian immersion> by a <Morse function> on $S^1$. Its minimum and maximum give two transverse double points. Resolving both by <Lagrangian surgery> adds two one-handles and changes the Euler characteristic by $-4$. One neck may be glued with the orientation-reversing choice, so the resolved surface is nonorientable.

= Givental construction of nonorientable Lagrangian surfaces
{c}
{parent=Clean-circle resolution of a Lagrangian surface immersion}

For every $l\geq1$, take a plane immersed circle with $l$ transverse double points and form its product with an embedded circle in a second symplectic plane. The resulting torus is a <product Lagrangian immersion> with $l$ clean double circles. Resolving them gives a connected nonorientable <Lagrangian embedding> in $\mathbb R^4$ with Euler characteristic $-4l$.

= Cotangent bundle
{title2=$T^*L$}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}
{wiki}

The cotangent bundle has the canonical one-form $\lambda_{(q,p)}(v)=p(d\pi(v))$ and the canonical symplectic form $d\lambda$ up to a conventional sign. Its zero section is a <Lagrangian submanifold>.

= Meromorphic n-differential
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{title2=$\omega\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{mer}}(K_X^{\otimes n})$}

On a <Riemann surface>, a meromorphic n-differential is a meromorphic section of the $n$th power of the <canonical bundle>. In a coordinate it is $F(z)(dz)^n$, with coefficients transforming by the $n$th power of the derivative. At a ramification coordinate $w=t^e$, its pullback has order $e\operatorname{ord}_w\omega+n(e-1)$. At a <modular cusp>, the exponential coordinate gives $dz=(h/(2\pi i))dq/q$, so a weight-$2n$ form of cusp order $v$ produces a differential of order $v-n$.

= Graph of a differential one-form
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{title2=$\Gamma_\theta=\{(x,\theta_x):x\in X\}$}

= One-form graph
{synonym}

A <differential one-form> $\theta$ is a smooth <section of a vector bundle> $T^*X\to X$. Its graph is its image as a section, and the projection restricts to a <diffeomorphism> onto $X$. Pullback of the <Liouville one-form> by that section equals $\theta$, making the <twisted Lagrangian graph criterion> immediate.

= Cotangent bundle orientation
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{title2=$(d\lambda)^n\ne0$}

Every <cotangent bundle> $T^*M$ is an <orientable smooth manifold> even when its base is not. The intrinsic <canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle> $\lambda_{(p,\alpha)}(W)=\alpha(d\pi W)$ has $d\lambda=\sum_i da_i\wedge dx^i$. Its $n$th wedge power is the nowhere-zero form $n!\,da_1\wedge dx^1\wedge\cdots\wedge da_n\wedge dx^n$, which defines an <orientation> of the $2n$-dimensional total space. Using $-d\lambda$ gives another standard sign convention; either is a <symplectic form>.

= Cotangent coordinate transition
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{title2=$b=(D_xy)^{-T}a$}

For coordinates $x,y$ on a <smooth manifold>, fibre coefficients of a <cotangent space> element obey $b_j=\sum_i a_i\partial x^i/\partial y^j$. Thus induced <cotangent bundle> charts change by $(x,a)\mapsto(y(x),b)$ with smooth, invertible, fibre-linear changes. The <canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle> satisfies $\sum_i a_i dx^i=\sum_j b_jdy^j$, and differentiating proves invariance of its <exterior derivative>.

= Canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{title2=$\lambda$}

= Liouville one-form
{c}
{synonym}

= Tautological one-form
{synonym}

= Canonical one-forms on cotangent bundles
{synonym}

For the <cotangent bundle> projection $\pi:T^*X\to X$, the canonical <differential one-form> is $\lambda_{(x,\xi)}(v)=\xi(d\pi(v))$. In coordinates it is $\sum_j\xi_jdx_j$. The canonical <symplectic form> is $-d\lambda$ in the position-first convention $\sum_jdx_j\wedge d\xi_j$.

= Cotangent fiber translation
{parent=Canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle}

Translation by a <differential one-form> $\beta$ is $(x,\xi)\mapsto(x,\xi+\beta_x)$. It changes the <canonical one-form on a cotangent bundle> by $\pi^*\beta$. A <closed differential form> gives a <symplectomorphism>; an <exact differential form> gives a <Hamiltonian diffeomorphism> when compact support is not required.

= Nontrivial exact fiber translation
{parent=Cotangent fiber translation}
{title2=$T_{dh}^*\omega=\omega,\quad T_{dh}^*\alpha=\alpha+\pi^*dh$}

If $dh$ is not identically zero, translating cotangent fibers by $dh$ preserves the canonical <symplectic form> but changes the <Liouville one-form> by $\pi^*dh$. Any positive-dimensional <smooth manifold> has a suitable nonconstant bump function in a chart. In dimension zero every <differential one-form> is zero, so this distinction cannot occur.

= Cotangent lift of a diffeomorphism
{title2=$f_\#$}
{parent=Cotangent bundle}

For a diffeomorphism $f:X\to X$, its cotangent lift is
$$
f_\#(x,\xi)=\bigl(f(x),(df_x^{-1})^*\xi\bigr).
$$
It preserves the canonical one-form and therefore the canonical symplectic form. Cotangent lift is functorial under composition.

= Symplectic cotangent lift with a closed momentum shift
{parent=Cotangent lift of a diffeomorphism}
{title2=$x=\chi(x'),\quad y=\eta\circ\chi+D\chi^{-T}y'$}

If $x=\chi(x')$ is an angular <diffeomorphism> and $\eta\cdot dx$ is a <closed differential one-form>, the map $y=\eta(\chi(x'))+D\chi(x')^{-T}y'$ is a <symplectomorphism>. It combines a <cotangent lift of a diffeomorphism> with translation by a <closed differential one-form>. In particular $\eta=du+a$ is allowed on a <torus> even when the constant one-form $a\cdot dx$ is not exact.

= Rigidity of cotangent Liouville-form preservation
{parent=Cotangent lift of a diffeomorphism}
{title2=$g^*\alpha=\alpha\Longrightarrow g=f_\#$}

= Liouville-form-preserving cotangent diffeomorphism
{c}
{synonym}

A <diffeomorphism> of the entire <cotangent bundle> preserving the <Liouville one-form> is the <cotangent lift of a diffeomorphism> of the base. It preserves the <zero section> and the <Liouville vector field>, hence commutes with the <cotangent fiber-dilation flow>. The limit as the fiber contracts forces it to cover its restriction to the <zero section>. Evaluation of the preserved one-form then forces the inverse-transpose derivative action on each covector.

= Conormal bundle
{title2=$N^*Y$}
{parent=Cotangent bundle}
{wiki}

For a submanifold $Y\subseteq X$, its conormal bundle consists of covectors annihilating $TY$. A cotangent lift sends $N^*Y$ to $N^*f(Y)$.

= Twisted cotangent symplectic form
{parent=Cotangent bundle}

If $\sigma$ is a closed two-form on $X$, then $\omega+\pi^*\sigma$ is a symplectic form on $T^*X$, called a twisted cotangent symplectic form. Nondegeneracy follows from the horizontal-vertical block form of the canonical symplectic form.

= Translation equivalence of exact twisted cotangent bundles
{parent=Twisted cotangent symplectic form}
{title2=$T_\beta^*\omega_\sigma=\omega$}

If $\sigma=d\beta$, <cotangent fiber translation> by $+\beta$ gives a <symplectomorphism> from $(T^*X,-d\alpha)$ to $(T^*X,-d\alpha+\pi^*\sigma)$. Its pullback changes $\alpha$ by $\pi^*\beta$, so the extra two-form cancels with $-\pi^*d\beta$. The opposite translation gives the inverse direction, globally without compactness.

= Twisted Lagrangian graph criterion
{parent=Twisted cotangent symplectic form}
{title2=$\theta^*\omega_\sigma=\sigma-d\theta$}

With $\omega=-d\alpha$ and $\omega_\sigma=\omega+\pi^*\sigma$, the <one-form graph> of $\theta$ is a <Lagrangian submanifold> exactly when $d\theta=\sigma$. Indeed its pulled-back <symplectic form> is $\sigma-d\theta$ and its dimension is half that of the total space. This extends <graph of a closed one-form is Lagrangian>.

= Cohomological obstruction to a Lagrangian section
{parent=Twisted Lagrangian graph criterion}
{title2=$[\sigma]\ne0\Longrightarrow\text{no global Lagrangian section}$}

If $[\sigma]\ne0$ in <de Rham cohomology>, a <twisted cotangent symplectic form> has no <Lagrangian submanifold> whose projection is a <diffeomorphism> onto the entire base. Such a submanifold would be a global <one-form graph>, and the <twisted Lagrangian graph criterion> would make $\sigma$ exact.

= Graph of a closed one-form is Lagrangian
{parent=Cotangent bundle}

The graph of a one-form $\beta$ in $T^*X$ is Lagrangian exactly when $d\beta=0$. Its intersections with the zero section are the zeros of $\beta$; if $\beta=df$, they are the critical points of $f$.

= Weinstein neighborhood theorem
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

= Weinstein neighborhood
{c}
{synonym}

A neighborhood of a compact <Lagrangian submanifold> $L\subset(M,\omega)$ is symplectomorphic, by a map restricting to the identity on $L$, to a neighborhood of the zero section in $(T^*L,d\lambda)$, with the sign chosen to match the convention for the canonical form.

= Symplectic splitting along a Lagrangian submanifold
{parent=Weinstein neighborhood theorem}
{title2=$TM|_X\cong TX\oplus T^*X$}

A <Lagrangian complement> to $TX$ in $TM|_X$ is identified with $T^*X$ by $v\mapsto\omega(\,\cdot\,,v)|_{TX}$. This produces a <vector bundle isomorphism> $TX\oplus T^*X\to TM|_X$ that is the identity on $TX$ and carries the canonical pairing $\zeta(u)-\eta(v)$ to $\omega$. A suitably chosen <tubular neighborhood> map therefore matches the ambient two-forms along $X$, enabling the <relative Moser theorem>.

= Nearby exact Lagrangian intersection lemma
{parent=Weinstein neighborhood theorem}

If a compact Lagrangian $L$ has $H^1(L;\mathbb R)=0$, every sufficiently close Lagrangian graph is the graph of $df$ and meets the zero section at least at a maximum and a minimum of $f$. Without the cohomology assumption, a nowhere-zero closed one-form can give a disjoint nearby graph.

= Compact first-cohomology obstruction to a Lagrangian fibration
{parent=Nearby exact Lagrangian intersection lemma}
{title2=$H^1(L;\mathbb R)=0$}

A compact <Lagrangian submanifold> $L$ with vanishing first <de Rham cohomology> cannot be a fibre of a smooth positive-dimensional <fiber bundle> whose nearby fibres are also <Lagrangian submanifolds>. In a <Weinstein neighborhood>, nearby fibres are <one-form graphs> of closed forms. They are exact by the <cohomology> assumption, so meet the <zero section> at a critical point of their potential, contradicting disjointness of distinct fibres.

= Hamiltonian flow preserves a constant-level Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

If a Hamiltonian is constant on a Lagrangian $L$, then $dH$ vanishes on $TL$. Hence its Hamiltonian vector field belongs to $(TL)^\omega=TL$, and its flow preserves $L$.

= Lagrangian displacement
{c}
{disambiguate=symplectic geometry}
{parent=Lagrangian submanifold}

A Lagrangian $L\subset M$ is displaceable by a class of isotopies when some isotopy $\phi_t$ in that class satisfies $\phi_1(L)\cap L=\varnothing$.

= Smooth non-displaceability from self-intersection
{parent=Lagrangian displacement (symplectic geometry)}

For a compact orientable Lagrangian $L$, the identification $NL\cong T^*L$ gives
$$
[L]\cdot[L]=\pm\langle e(TL),[L]\rangle=\pm\chi(L).
$$
If $\chi(L)\ne0$, no smoothly isotopic copy of $L$ can be disjoint from it.

= Symplectic non-displaceability of an area bisector
{parent=Lagrangian displacement (symplectic geometry)}

A simple closed curve dividing a symplectic two-sphere into two regions of equal area cannot be displaced by a <symplectic isotopy>. Any disjoint image would lie in one complementary disc, yet it would still have to bound a disc of half the total area.

= Moser's trick
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

= Moser theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Let $M$ be compact and let $\omega_t$ be a smooth family of symplectic forms with constant <de Rham cohomology> class. Choose $\sigma_t$ smoothly so that $\dot\omega_t=d\sigma_t$, solve
$$
\iota_{X_t}\omega_t=-\sigma_t,
$$
and let $\phi_t$ be the flow of $X_t$. Then
$$
\frac d{dt}(\phi_t^*\omega_t)
=\phi_t^*(\dot\omega_t+\mathcal L_{X_t}\omega_t)=0,
$$
so $\phi_t^*\omega_t=\omega_0$.

= Relative Moser theorem
{parent=Moser's trick}
{title2=$\kappa^*\omega_1=\omega_0,\quad\kappa|_S=\operatorname{id}_S$}

Two closed <symplectic forms> agreeing as ambient bilinear forms along a compact embedded submanifold are equivalent near it by a <diffeomorphism> fixing it pointwise. Shrink until their affine interpolation is <nondegenerate>. A relative homotopy primitive $\eta$ for their difference vanishes as an ambient covector on the submanifold. Solving $\iota_{Z_t}\omega_t=-\eta$ gives a <vector field> vanishing there; its local flow fixes the submanifold and makes $\omega_t$ constant under pullback.

= Moser theorem for volume forms
{c}
{parent=Moser's trick}

On a <compact> <connected> <smooth manifold> without boundary, two positive <volume forms> for the same <orientation> are related by an <orientation>-preserving <diffeomorphism> exactly when their total integrals agree. A positive interpolation and <Cartan's magic formula> give the <smooth isotopy> realizing the equality.

= Symplectic equivalence of smooth projective hypersurfaces
{parent=Moser's trick}

Any two smooth degree-$d$ hypersurfaces in $\mathbb{CP}^n$, equipped with the restricted <Fubini-Study form>, are symplectomorphic. Join them through the connected complement of the discriminant in the parameter space, use the resulting smooth family to identify the fibers, and apply <Moser's trick> to the cohomologous restricted forms.

= Fermat hypersurface
{c}
{parent=Symplectic equivalence of smooth projective hypersurfaces}
{wiki}

The degree-$d$ Fermat hypersurface in $\mathbb{CP}^n$ is
$$
z_0^d+\cdots+z_n^d=0.
$$

= Fermat hypersurface diagonal symmetry
{c}
{parent=Fermat hypersurface}

The Fermat hypersurface $z_0^d+\cdots+z_n^d=0$ is preserved by multiplying each coordinate by a $d$th root of unity. Projective scalar multiplication is trivial, so the effective diagonal group is
$$
(\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z)^{n+1}/\langle(1,\ldots,1)\rangle\cong(\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z)^n.
$$
These transformations preserve the <Fubini-Study form>.

= Symplectic submanifold
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

A submanifold $S\subset(M,\omega)$ is symplectic when $\omega|_{TS}$ is nondegenerate.

= Symplectic smoothing of a positive transverse node
{parent=Symplectic submanifold}

Two symplectic surfaces meeting positively and transversely can be replaced near an intersection by a smooth symplectic surface representing the sum of their homology classes. In compatible complex coordinates the local union is $\{xy=0\}$ and its smoothing is $\{xy=\epsilon\}$; a cutoff joins this local model to the original surfaces.

= Symplectic adjunction formula
{parent=Symplectic submanifold}

For a connected embedded symplectic surface $C$ in a symplectic four-manifold,
$$
2g(C)-2=C^2+K_\omega\mathbin{\cdot}C,
$$
where $K_\omega=-c_1(TM,J)$ is the symplectic canonical class for any <compatible almost complex structure>.

= Positive intersection of J-holomorphic curves
{title2=$C_1\mathbin{\cdot}C_2\geq0$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic submanifold}

Distinct simple $J$-holomorphic curves in an almost complex four-manifold have positive local intersection multiplicities. Consequently their homological intersection number is nonnegative whenever they intersect in finitely many points.

= Symplectic normal bundle
{parent=Symplectic submanifold}

The symplectic normal bundle of $S$ is the symplectic orthogonal complement $(TS)^\omega\subset TM|_S$. It is a symplectic vector bundle and is naturally isomorphic to the ordinary normal bundle.

= Symplectic neighborhood theorem
{parent=Symplectic normal bundle}

If a symplectomorphism between compact symplectic submanifolds lifts to an isomorphism of their <symplectic normal bundles>, then it extends to a symplectomorphism between neighborhoods. Thus the germ of a symplectic neighborhood is determined by the restricted form and the symplectic normal bundle.

= Symplectic sum
{parent=Symplectic submanifold}
{wiki}

A symplectic sum removes tubular neighborhoods of symplectic submanifolds whose normal bundles have opposite <Euler classes> and glues their boundaries by a fiber-reversing identification.

= Symplectic rational blowdown of a minus-four sphere
{parent=Symplectic sum}

A symplectic sphere of self-intersection $-4$ can be rationally blown down by taking the <symplectic sum> with $\mathbb{CP}^2$ along the sphere and a smooth conic of self-intersection $+4$. Equivalently, its disk-bundle neighborhood is replaced by the rational ball $\mathbb{CP}^2\setminus\nu(Q)$ whose boundary is the lens space $L(4,1)$.

= Pseudoholomorphic curve
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

= J-holomorphic curve
{c}
{synonym}

For almost complex manifolds $(\Sigma,j)$ and $(X,J)$, a smooth map $u:\Sigma\to X$ is J-holomorphic when $du\circ j=J\circ du$, equivalently $\bar\partial_Ju=0$.

= Removal of a finite-energy holomorphic puncture
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}

A <J-holomorphic curve> on a punctured disc with finite energy and image contained in a compact target extends smoothly across the puncture when the target has a smooth tame <almost complex structure> and compatible bounded geometry. Local monotonicity controls escape from small target balls, and the elliptic small-energy estimate followed by <elliptic regularity> gives the extension. This is the step turning a finite-energy plane obtained by rescaling into a <J-holomorphic sphere>.

= Simple J-holomorphic curve
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}

A <J-holomorphic curve> is simple if it does not factor through a holomorphic branched covering of degree greater than one. For a nonconstant curve this is equivalent to having a point where its derivative is nonzero and its image has exactly one preimage.

= Simple J-holomorphic sphere
{parent=Simple J-holomorphic curve}

A simple J-holomorphic sphere is a <J-holomorphic sphere> that is a <simple J-holomorphic curve>. Its automorphism group as a parametrized map is trivial, because any automorphism fixing the map fixes its locally injective points.

= Adjunction inequality for a simple J-holomorphic sphere
{parent=Simple J-holomorphic sphere}
{title2=$c_1(A)\leq A^2+2$}

The singular-curve adjunction formula in a <symplectic manifold> of real dimension four gives $c_1(A)=A^2+2-2\delta$ for a <simple J-holomorphic sphere>, with a nonnegative singularity count $\delta$. Hence the displayed inequality holds. This is the version needed when the sphere need not be embedded.

= J-holomorphic sphere
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}
{c}
{title2=$u:\mathbb{CP}^1\to M$}

A J-holomorphic sphere is a <J-holomorphic curve> with domain the <complex projective line>.

= Gromov compactness for spheres
{parent=J-holomorphic sphere}
{c}

For a compact <symplectic manifold>, smooth convergence of tame <almost complex structures> and a uniform area bound give a subsequence of <J-holomorphic spheres> converging, after reparametrization, to a finite tree of <J-holomorphic spheres>, with convergence smooth away from finitely many bubbling points. The total <homology class> and <symplectic area> are preserved. Constant components may be added for stability. In a spherical class which cannot split into two positive-area spherical classes, there is only one nonconstant component.

= Fibre spheres in a sphere-torus product
{parent=Gromov compactness for spheres}
{title2=$F=[S^2\times\{q\}]$}

In a product <symplectic manifold> $S^2(a)\times T^{2n-2}$, every <compatible almost complex structure> admits a <J-holomorphic sphere> in class $F$ through each point. Here $S^2(a)$ has area $a$, and the <torus> carries a constant <symplectic form>. All spherical classes are integer multiples of $F$, so a curve in class $F$ cannot split into positive-area nonconstant spheres. For the product <complex structure> the fibre through a point is unique and regular. Its one-point count modulo two is one; generic <transversality> and the compact one-dimensional parameterized <moduli spaces> preserve this count. Approximation and <Gromov compactness for spheres> remove the genericity requirement. This gives the area-$a$ curve used in the proof of the <Gromov non-squeezing theorem>.

= Dimension formula for regular J-holomorphic spheres
{parent=J-holomorphic sphere}
{title2=$\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathcal M_A=2n+2c_1(A)-6$}

For a regular nonconstant <J-holomorphic sphere> in a $2n$-dimensional target, the parametrized <Fredholm index> is $2n+2c_1(A)$. Removing the six-dimensional <Möbius transformation> reparametrization group gives the displayed local moduli dimension. The quotient is a manifold for a <simple J-holomorphic sphere> and may be an orbifold for a multiple cover with finite stabilizer. In dimension four the formula is $2c_1(A)-2$.

= Cauchy–Riemann operator
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}
{c}
{title2=$\bar\partial_Ju=\tfrac12(du+J\circ du\circ j)$}

This first-order operator vanishes exactly on <J-holomorphic curves>. Linearization at a map $u$ gives a <Fredholm operator> between suitable <Sobolev spaces> of sections. At a constant map it is the <Dolbeault operator> on the trivial pulled-back tangent bundle.

= Riemann-Roch index for a real Cauchy-Riemann operator
{parent=Cauchy–Riemann operator}
{c}
{title2=$\operatorname{ind}_{\mathbb R}D=2n(1-g)+2c_1(E)[\Sigma]$}

A real-linear <Cauchy–Riemann operator> on a complex rank-$n$ <vector bundle> over a closed genus-$g$ <Riemann surface> is a <Fredholm operator> between the usual <Sobolev spaces> and has the displayed index. Zero-order real-linear terms do not change this index, since they form a continuous deformation of <Fredholm operators>. For the bundle pulled back by a sphere, subtracting the six-dimensional reparametrization group gives the <dimension formula for regular J-holomorphic spheres>.

= Regularity of a J-holomorphic curve
{parent=Cauchy–Riemann operator}
{title2=$D_u\text{ is surjective}$}

A <J-holomorphic curve> is regular when the linearized <Cauchy–Riemann operator> is <surjective>. Regularity only for simple curves is weaker than regularity for all maps: multiple covers of an exceptional sphere can fail regularity even when that simple sphere is regular.

= Generic transversality for simple holomorphic spheres
{parent=Regularity of a J-holomorphic curve}

For a residual set of smooth <compatible almost complex structures>, all <simple J-holomorphic spheres> in a specified countable set of spherical classes are regular. The corresponding evaluation maps at marked points are transverse to generic incidence conditions; generic paths give parameterized <transversality>. The universal linearization can vary the target structure near an <injective> point of the curve, which removes its <cokernel>. This assertion concerns simple curves and does not ensure regularity of arbitrary multiple covers.

= Negative-square exclusion under full sphere regularity
{parent=Regularity of a J-holomorphic curve}

If every nonconstant <J-holomorphic sphere> under consideration is regular, no such sphere in a four-dimensional target has class $A$ with $A^2\leq-2$. For a <simple J-holomorphic sphere>, the <adjunction inequality for a simple J-holomorphic sphere> makes the predicted moduli dimension negative. A degree-$m$ multiple cover of class $B$ has $B^2\leq-1$ and $c_1(B)\leq1$, so its predicted real dimension is at most $2m-2$. The <rational covering maps of the projective line> already form a family of dimension $4m-4>2m-2$, contradicting regularity. The full regularity hypothesis matters: multiple covers of exceptional minus-one spheres violate the conclusion under a simple-only regularity assumption.

= Lagrangian boundary condition
{c}
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}
{title2=$u(s,i)\in L_i$}

A Lagrangian boundary condition for a <J-holomorphic curve> requires specified boundary components of the source to map into specified <Lagrangian submanifolds>. For the strip, this is $u(s,0)\in L_0$ and $u(s,1)\in L_1$. For a <compatible almost complex structure> these target submanifolds are totally real. The condition accompanies the interior equation $u_s+Ju_t=0$.

= Energy identity for a J-holomorphic curve
{c}
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}

For compatible metrics on a Riemann surface and symplectic target,
$$
E(u)=\frac12\int_\Sigma|du|^2
=\int_\Sigma u^*\omega+\int_\Sigma|\bar\partial_Ju|^2.
$$
Thus a J-holomorphic curve minimizes energy among maps in its homology class.

= Monotonicity theorem for a J-holomorphic curve
{c}
{parent=Pseudoholomorphic curve}

On a compact region with controlled compatible almost complex structure, there are $c,r_0>0$ such that a nonconstant J-holomorphic curve through the center of a ball of radius $r<r_0$, with boundary outside the ball, has symplectic area at least $cr^2$. For the standard complex structure the sharp local constant is $\pi$.

= Non-squeezing theorem
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

= Gromov non-squeezing theorem
{c}
{synonym}

If there is a symplectic embedding
$$
B^{2n}(R)\hookrightarrow B^2(r)\times\mathbb R^{2n-2},
$$
then $R\leq r$. Symplectic volume alone does not imply this radius obstruction.

= Arbitrarily large symplectic balls in a cotangent cylinder
{parent=Non-squeezing theorem}

The symplectic manifold $T^2\times\mathbb R^2$ contains symplectic balls of arbitrarily large radius. A steep linear Lagrangian graph in $\mathbb R^4=T^*\mathbb R^2$ has a thin neighborhood that injects under the quotient $\mathbb R^2\to T^2$, while a symplectic base-fiber dilation fits a large round ball into that long thin neighborhood.

= Symplectic fiber sum along a square-zero surface
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

If symplectic four-manifolds contain symplectic copies of the same closed surface with self-intersection zero, their trivial symplectic normal bundles may be identified with reversed circle fibers. Removing the two tubular neighborhoods and gluing their boundaries gives a <symplectic sum> carrying a natural symplectic form.

= Gompf realization theorem
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

Every finitely presented group is the <fundamental group> of a closed symplectic four-manifold. The construction starts from products of surfaces, realizes relator words by embedded loops after stabilization, and takes <symplectic sums> with copies of the <rational elliptic surface> along square-zero tori. Since the complement of a regular elliptic fiber in the rational elliptic surface is simply connected, van Kampen's theorem imposes the desired generators and relators.

= Gromov width
{title2=$c_G$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}
{wiki}

The Gromov width of a symplectic manifold is the supremum of $\pi r^2$ over radii of symplectically embedded balls $B^{2n}(r)$.

= Gromov width of the monotone product of projective lines
{title2=$c_G(\mathbb{CP}^1\times\mathbb{CP}^1)=\pi$}
{c}
{parent=Gromov width}

When each projective line has area $\pi$, the product $\mathbb{CP}^1\times\mathbb{CP}^1$ has Gromov width $\pi$. The lower bound comes from the product of two area-$\pi$ discs; the upper bound comes from a J-holomorphic sphere of area $\pi$ through the center of any proposed ball.

= Liouville class of a Lagrangian submanifold
{title2=$[\lambda|_L]$}
{c}
{parent=Symplectic manifold}

For a Lagrangian submanifold $L\subset(\mathbb R^{2n},d\lambda)$, the restriction $\lambda|_L$ is closed. Its cohomology class is preserved by ambient symplectomorphisms because every closed one-form on $\mathbb R^{2n}$ is exact. On a product torus of circles, its periods are the enclosed symplectic areas of the factors.

= Holomorphic disk detects a nonzero Liouville class
{parent=Liouville class of a Lagrangian submanifold}

For a smooth nonconstant holomorphic disk $h$ in $\mathbb C^n$ with boundary on a <Lagrangian submanifold> $L$, its symplectic area is $\int_{D^2}|\partial_sh|^2\,ds\,dt>0$. The <Cauchy-Riemann equations> give $\partial_th=J\partial_sh$, so this is the pullback of the standard <symplectic form>. The <Stokes theorem> identifies the area with the period of a primitive along the boundary loop. Thus the <Liouville class of a Lagrangian submanifold> evaluates nontrivially on this loop and cannot vanish.

= Rational Lagrangian submanifold
{parent=Liouville class of a Lagrangian submanifold}

For a <Lagrangian submanifold> $L$ in an exact symplectic manifold with primitive $\lambda$, its period subgroup is $\{\int_c\lambda:c\in H_1(L;\mathbb Z)\}\subset\mathbb R$. It is rational when this subgroup is discrete. If it is nonzero, it is $\gamma\mathbb Z$ for a unique $\gamma>0$. Some conventions reserve the word rational for this nonzero case. The zero subgroup corresponds to an <exact differential form> $\lambda|_L$; its least positive period is interpreted as $+\infty$ if that endpoint convention is admitted.

= Least positive Liouville period
{parent=Rational Lagrangian submanifold}
{title2=$\gamma(L)=\inf\{a>0:a\in\operatorname{Per}(L)\}$}

For a nonzero discrete Liouville period group, this is its positive generator. In Euclidean symplectic space, the same group is obtained from signed symplectic areas of disks with boundary on $L$, by the <Stokes theorem> and contractibility of the ambient space. A product of circles of common radius $r$ is a <rational Lagrangian submanifold> with $\gamma(L)=\pi r^2$. Translation changes a primitive only by an exact form and hence leaves the period group unchanged.
